{"doc_id":"doc-026c11253dbd","journal_id":"journal-045-international-review-of-research-in-open-and-dis","journal_name":"International Review of Research in Open and Distributed Learning","source_url":"https://www.irrodl.org/index.php/irrodl/article/view/10083","source_type":"article","title":"Determining Accountability in AI-Assisted Scholarship: A Report and Recommendations","captured_at":"2026-09-29T01:21:20+00:00","text_snippet":" Determining Accountability in AI-Assisted Scholarship: A Report and Recommendations | The International Review of Research in Open and Distributed Learning Open Menu The International Review of Research in Open and Distributed Learning Determining Accountability in AI-Assisted Scholarship: A Report and Recommendations Authors Jim O'Driscoll Independent Researcher https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3765-1259 Rory McGreal UNESCO Chair in OER, Athabasca University, Canada DOI: https://doi.org/10.19173/irrodl.v27i3.10083 Keywords: generative AI, GenAI, accountability, authorship, scholarly publishing, Goffman, production format, open access Abstract The proliferation of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) presents a significant challenge to traditional notions of accountability in scholarly publishing. This report addresses the pressing question of who is responsible for the content of academic work when its creation involves AI assistance. It does so by introducing and extending Erving Goffman’s (1981a, b) concept of production format , a framework that deconstructs the singular speaker into three distinct roles: animator , author (herein re-labelled designer ), and principal . By applying this framework to written, scholarly communication, this report analyzes two key problems: the accountability of publishers and platforms as relaying animators and the apparent displacement of "}
