{"doc_id":"doc-4ba2dbb7d677","journal_id":"journal-045-international-review-of-research-in-open-and-dis","journal_name":"International Review of Research in Open and Distributed Learning","source_url":"https://www.irrodl.org/index.php/irrodl/article/view/8805","source_type":"article","title":"Blended Learning in a Post-Pandemic Context: A Cross-Sectional Study on Turkish EFL Learners’ Perspectives","captured_at":"2026-09-29T01:21:20+00:00","text_snippet":" Blended Learning in a Post-Pandemic Context: A Cross-Sectional Study on Turkish EFL Learners’ Perspectives | The International Review of Research in Open and Distributed Learning Open Menu The International Review of Research in Open and Distributed Learning Blended Learning in a Post-Pandemic Context: A Cross-Sectional Study on Turkish EFL Learners’ Perspectives Authors Burak Tomak Marmara University, Istanbul, Turkiye https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6678-431X Ayşe Yılmaz Virlan Marmara University, Istanbul, Turkiye https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6839-5745 DOI: https://doi.org/10.19173/irrodl.v27i3.8805 Keywords: blended learning, cross-sectional research, digital maturity, EFL learners, learner autonomy, student engagement Abstract During the COVID-19 pandemic, various institutions took measures to prevent the spread of the virus. Educational institutions contributed to these efforts by shifting from face-to-face to online learning. One such approach was blended learning, which served as a transitional model between the two. Blended learning has become an integral part of higher education, particularly in English as a foreign language (EFL) instruction. This study investigated Turkish EFL learners’ perceptions of blended learning by comparing two different student cohorts from 2021–2022 and 2024–2025 using a cross-sectional research design. Data were collected through classroom obs"}
