{"doc_id":"doc-967ac1c50ba3","journal_id":"journal-045-international-review-of-research-in-open-and-dis","journal_name":"International Review of Research in Open and Distributed Learning","source_url":"https://www.irrodl.org/index.php/irrodl/article/view/8858","source_type":"article","title":"Predicting Student Outcomes in Open High Schools Using Educational Data Mining","captured_at":"2026-09-29T01:21:20+00:00","text_snippet":" Predicting Student Outcomes in Open High Schools Using Educational Data Mining | The International Review of Research in Open and Distributed Learning Open Menu The International Review of Research in Open and Distributed Learning Predicting Student Outcomes in Open High Schools Using Educational Data Mining Authors Ahmet Polat Ministry of National Education, Sivas, Türkiye https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0615-3732 Mehmet Barış Horzum Department of Computer Education and Instructional Technology, Faculty of Education, Sakarya University, Adapazarı, Türkiye https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3567-0779 DOI: https://doi.org/10.19173/irrodl.v27i3.8858 Keywords: educational data mining, dropout prediction, open high school, machine learning, distance education, student retention, early warning system Abstract Open high schools fulfill a critical function by offering flexible educational pathways for students encountering diverse socioeconomic and personal challenges. Nevertheless, escalating dropout rates represent a significant concern, necessitating robust predictive models to facilitate early interventions. This study employed educational data mining (EDM) techniques to analyze the academic trajectories of 484,158 students enrolled in Turkish open high schools. We evaluated multiple classification algorithms—J48, decision tree, k-nearest neighbors (kNN), naïve Bayes, and random forest—acro"}
